    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gastric ulcer with recent bleeding (K25.4)</content>. Endoscopy shows a 15 mm ulcer on the lesser curvature at the angulus with a clean white base (Forrest III), regular margins and smooth converging folds, which look benign; there is no blood in the stomach and the duodenum is normal. A clean base carries a low risk of rebleeding, and with a stable hemoglobin of 11.0 g/dL and normal pulse and blood pressure he can be treated at home. Six biopsies of the ulcer margin were taken because a gastric ulcer can hide a cancer, which is common in Japan. Dr. Abrahamsen-Ito and I agree the plan. [S]<list><item>Vonoprazan 20 mg each morning for eight weeks, started after the biopsies.</item><item>No NSAIDs; acetaminophen for pain.</item><item>Call 119 for black, tarry stools, vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, or fainting.</item><item>Biopsy results and the eradication plan at two weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Helicobacter pylori infection (B96.81)</content>. The rapid urease test on an antral biopsy is positive. The background mucosa shows open-type atrophy with antral nodularity. Mapping biopsies for histology and a PCR test for clarithromycin resistance were sent; in Japan clarithromycin resistance is common, so treatment will be chosen when the result is back. [S]<list><item>Eradication therapy at the two-week visit, chosen by the resistance result.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Iron-deficiency anemia (D50.0)</content>. Hemoglobin 11.0 g/dL, stable; the ulcer explains the blood loss. [S]<list><item>Ferrous sulfate 325 mg every other morning from today, now that the source has been seen.</item><item>Iron turns the stools dark grey-green or black but formed; black, sticky, tarry stools are different and mean bleeding.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper endoscopy with biopsy of the ulcer margin</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat for a gastric ulcer at 8 to 12 weeks to confirm healing and biopsy the scar</td><td>Completed today</td><td>15 mm clean-based gastric ulcer at the angulus (Forrest III); six margin biopsies sent</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Helicobacter pylori test (rapid urease test and histology) with clarithromycin-resistance testing</td><td>At diagnosis, before any acid suppression or antibiotic</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Rapid urease test positive; histology and clarithromycin-resistance PCR sent</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin before endoscopy</td><td>At endoscopy for a recent bleed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>11.0 g/dL, stable from 11.2</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acid suppression for ulcer healing (vonoprazan)</td><td>Daily for eight weeks for a gastric ulcer</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Vonoprazan 20 mg each morning started after the biopsies</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID avoidance</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ibuprofen stopped at the first visit; none since</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Bleeding warning signs and when to call 119</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No new black stools; warning signs and 119 reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Alcohol, smoking and meals</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Does not smoke; alcohol rarely; regular meals advised</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral iron</td><td>From the day the source is found</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ferrous sulfate every other morning started today</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Stress and depression: PHQ-9 and GAD-7</td><td>At intake and at the end of the episode</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 7 and GAD-7 8 three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Gastroenterology consultation and endoscopy</td><td>Within three days for a recent ulcer bleed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Abrahamsen-Ito this morning</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Endoscopy within three days of the first visit, with cancer excluded by biopsy. In progress; target 1910-06-04 [S]</item><item>No further bleeding and no emergency admission through the episode. In progress; target 1910-08-03 [S]</item><item>Helicobacter pylori eradicated, confirmed by urea breath test. In progress; target 1910-08-03 [S]</item><item>Gastric ulcer healed at repeat endoscopy. In progress; target 1910-08-03 [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin normal and ferritin above 50 ng/mL. In progress; target 1910-08-03 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection (2024); Management of Helicobacter pylori infection: the Maastricht VI/Florence consensus report (2022); Guidelines for the management of Helicobacter pylori infection in Japan: 2016 Revised Edition (2016 (published 2019)); Evidence-based clinical practice guidelines for peptic ulcer disease 2026 (2026); ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Two-week visit with the biopsy results, the resistance result, the eradication plan and a hemoglobin. Call 119 for black, tarry stools, vomiting blood or fainting. Next scheduled visit 1910-06-04: two-week review: biopsy results, Helicobacter pylori resistance testing, and start of eradication therapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
